CARDS = [
# ---------- Node 0 ----------
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "Why are computers binary rather than decimal, given that a wire can carry any voltage?", "Because of <strong>noise</strong>. A wire's voltage sags and wobbles by hundredths of a volt, which is fatal if digits are a tenth of a volt apart — and nothing downstream can detect the error. With only two legal values separated by a wide no-man's-land, noise is discarded at every step instead of accumulating. You give up nine tenths of the wire's information capacity and gain that every value is exactly right or catastrophically wrong, never slightly wrong."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "Which real machine worked in decimal, and what does that prove?", "<strong>ENIAC</strong> (1945, University of Pennsylvania) stored each digit as a ring of ten circuits with one lit. It proves binary was a live engineering choice that won a fight, not an obvious truth discovered in hindsight."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "What is a transistor, in terms of what it does rather than what it is made of?", "A switch operated by electricity rather than by a finger: three connections — input, output, control. Voltage on the control lets current flow. Demonstrated at Bell Labs in December 1947 by Bardeen and Brattain, with Shockley's theory. A phone chip has over ten billion and nothing else."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "How do switches become arithmetic?", "Two transistors in series is an <strong>AND</strong>; in parallel an <strong>OR</strong>; one wired inverted is a <strong>NOT</strong>. Those three build everything. Addition is an XOR for the sum plus an AND for the carry (a half adder), chained so each carry feeds the next. Shannon's 1937 MIT thesis showed switching circuits and Boolean algebra are the same subject."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "What is a register, and why are there only a few dozen?", "A row of flip-flops holding one number, sitting physically adjacent to the arithmetic circuits — a signal travels a fraction of a millimetre, so it is the fastest storage in the machine. You cannot have millions because there is no room and the wires would be long enough for delay to dominate."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "Why is main memory built from a different technology than registers?", "DRAM uses one transistor and one tiny capacitor per bit, so it is far denser — billions of cells. The capacitor leaks, which is why it must be refreshed thousands of times a second (the D is for dynamic). The trade is density for speed: roughly 80 nanoseconds versus effectively zero."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "What, physically, is a memory address?", "A number naming one of billions of storage cells. Memory is literally a very long row of numbered boxes each holding a byte, with exactly two operations: load box N into a register, store a register into box N. Every data structure you have ever used is made of those two."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "Why is a byte eight bits?", "A decision, not a law. IBM's System/360 (April 1964) standardised eight because it held one character in IBM's encoding and because eight is a power of two, making address arithmetic a bit-shift rather than a division. Earlier machines used six, seven and nine. Eight won because System/360 won."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "What is the single idea that turns a calculator into a computer?", "Putting instructions in the same memory as data, encoded as numbers — the stored-program design, written down in the <em>First Draft of a Report on the EDVAC</em> in June 1945 under von Neumann's name, recording work with Eckert and Mauchly."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "Name four consequences of instructions being data.", "Programs can be loaded rather than rewired (seconds, not days); programs can be stored on media, so software exists apart from a machine; a program can write a program, which is what a compiler and V8's JIT are; and a program can overwrite itself or another, which is the root of the buffer-overflow class of vulnerability."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "What is an instruction set architecture, and why does it matter to a Docker user?", "A fixed agreement mapping bit patterns to operations, wired into the processor. x86-64, ARM64 and RISC-V are mutually unintelligible agreements, which is why a compiled binary for one will not run on the other, why images carry an architecture tag, and why <code>exec format error</code> happens."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "State the entire operation of a processor in four steps.", "Fetch the bytes at the address in <code>rip</code>; decode what that pattern means; execute it; advance <code>rip</code> by the instruction's length. Repeat forever. There is no notion of a program, a user, a file or a scheduler in the hardware — all of those are software."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "What is a jump, mechanically?", "A write to the instruction pointer. An <code>if</code> is a comparison plus a conditional write to <code>rip</code>; a loop is a backward write; a call is a write plus a note of where to return. Control flow is arithmetic on one register."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "The fetch-decode-execute loop never asks anything and never pauses. Why does that matter for the rest of operating systems?", "Because everything after it is an answer to: given a device with total obedience and no ability to notice a better use of its time, how do you get it to do more than one thing, safely, for more than one person?"),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "What do pipelining, speculative execution and out-of-order execution change about the model?", "Nothing observable — they are optimisations the processor is contractually required to make invisible. They matter because speculative execution's cache side effects are what Spectre exploits, which is why <code>performance.now()</code> was coarsened and <code>SharedArrayBuffer</code> now needs cross-origin isolation headers."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "What exactly is a JavaScript stack trace, at the machine level?", "A chain of saved instruction pointer values walked through memory, with each address mapped back to a function name using a table the runtime kept. That is why a stripped native binary gives hexadecimal, why source maps exist, and why collecting frames costs real time."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "Why does a native Node addon compiled on an Apple laptop fail inside a linux/amd64 container?", "A <code>.node</code> file is compiled machine code for one ISA. Loading it on another gives <code>invalid ELF header</code>. This is the actual reason host <code>node_modules</code> must never be copied into an image and must be installed inside the build."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "What is endianness and where does it surface in JavaScript?", "Which end of a multi-byte number is stored at the lower address. x86 and ARM are little-endian; network protocols conventionally use big-endian, hence <em>network byte order</em>. It surfaces as <code>buf.readUInt32BE</code> versus <code>readUInt32LE</code>."),
("node-0", "Node 0 · The bare machine", "What does the machine that runs your JavaScript actually execute?", "Machine code. V8 parses your source into a tree, interprets it with Ignition, and for hot functions hands it to TurboFan (and since 2021 Sparkplug), which <em>writes machine code into memory and jumps to it</em> — the 1945 stored-program property used at run time, thousands of times per process."),
# ---------- Node 1 ----------
("node-1", "Node 1 · The resident monitor", "What was the situation Node 0 left, in economic terms?", "The most expensive object in the building sat idle for most of every hour, waiting on humans walking across rooms, on card readers, and on the gap between one person's booked slot and the next. Every mechanism in operating systems is a response to some version of that observation."),
("node-1", "Node 1 · The resident monitor", "What is a resident monitor?", "A program that stays in memory while jobs come and go, reading the next job, loading it, jumping to it, and regaining control when it finishes. It is the first operating system, and it is a loop."),
("node-1", "Node 1 · The resident monitor", "What was the first operating system, and what were its two halves?", "<strong>GM-NAA I/O</strong>, 1956, by Robert Patrick (General Motors) and Owen Mock (North American Aviation) for the IBM 704. GM-NAA is the batch monitor loop; I/O is a set of shared input/output subroutines so every program did not rewrite card and tape handling itself."),
("node-1", "Node 1 · The resident monitor", "What two lasting concepts came out of the I/O half of GM-NAA I/O?", "The <strong>device driver</strong> — code that knows one piece of hardware's bit-level protocol, written once — and the <strong>system library</strong>, shared code the system provides. The reason you write <code>fs.readFile</code> instead of issuing NVMe commands is a straight line from 1956."),
("node-1", "Node 1 · The resident monitor", "Why did job control cards have to be separate from the program?", "The monitor must read them <em>before</em> the job runs, and it may not be able to parse the job's language at all. So resource limits, accounts, and step definitions live in a small separate language — IBM's JCL, from OS/360 in 1964."),
("node-1", "Node 1 · The resident monitor", "Map a JCL job to a GitHub Actions workflow.", "<code>JOB</code> is the job; account and <code>CLASS=</code> are billing and <code>runs-on</code>; <code>TIME=(,30)</code> is <code>timeout-minutes</code>; <code>EXEC</code> steps are <code>steps</code>; <code>DD</code> statements declaring inputs and outputs are artefacts and <code>needs</code>. It is a pipeline definition file with worse syntax and better error messages."),
("node-1", "Node 1 · The resident monitor", "What is spooling and what general principle does it establish?", "<em>Simultaneous Peripheral Operations On-Line</em>: while job N computes, job N+1's input is read to disk and job N−1's output is printed, all concurrently. The principle: when a fast producer meets a slow consumer, put a queue between them and let each run at its own rate."),
("node-1", "Node 1 · The resident monitor", "What was <em>offline</em> operation and why was it done?", "Using a cheap slow machine (an IBM 1401) to copy card decks onto tape, then carrying the tape to the expensive machine, which could read it far faster. It kept the expensive processor off the card reader's clock."),
("node-1", "Node 1 · The resident monitor", "In what precise sense is a CI runner a resident monitor?", "It is a process that stays alive polling a queue, receives a job, prepares an environment, runs its steps, reports status, tears down, and returns to polling. Patrick and Mock's loop with HTTPS where the tape drive was."),
("node-1", "Node 1 · The resident monitor", "Name three BullMQ design features that are 1956 batch-monitor problems.", "The stalled-job checker with a renewable lock is the crashed-worker problem; <code>attempts</code> and <code>backoff</code> are JCL's <code>COND</code> codes; the job timeout is <code>TIME=</code>. They are not analogous problems — they are the same problems."),
("node-1", "Node 1 · The resident monitor", "What did 1950s spooling not have to solve that your streams do?", "<strong>Backpressure with an unbounded producer.</strong> The tape was finite. An HTTP client reading a response slowly while your server writes fast is an unbounded queue in your heap — which is why <code>write()</code> returning <code>false</code> and the <code>drain</code> event exist and why ignoring them grows memory until the process is killed."),
("node-1", "Node 1 · The resident monitor", "Why does every real cron job need a lock?", "If a run takes longer than the interval, the next starts anyway and you have two. Kubernetes exposes this as <code>concurrencyPolicy: Forbid</code>. The problem is inherent to the batch model, not to cron."),
("node-1", "Node 1 · The resident monitor", "What three things were still broken after the resident monitor?", "The processor still stopped dead whenever a job touched a device (polling); control returned to the monitor only if the job chose to return; and the monitor was ordinary bytes any job could overwrite."),
# ---------- Node 2 ----------
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "Give the ratio that motivates interrupts, in modern terms.", "A processor executes ~10 billion instructions per second. Main memory is ~80 ns away (hundreds of instructions), an SSD read ~100 µs (a million), a cross-continent round trip ~100 ms (a billion). The gap between the processor and everything else is the central physical fact of computing."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "Describe the interrupt mechanism in the fetch-decode-execute loop.", "After completing the current instruction — never halfway, since a half-executed instruction is unrecoverable — and before fetching the next, the processor checks the interrupt line. If raised: save <code>rip</code> and flags, look up the handler address by interrupt number, set <code>rip</code> to it. A return-from-interrupt instruction restores everything."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "What is asynchrony, physically?", "A wire, and a rule about checking it between instructions. Code ran in the middle of other code without anything calling it, and the interrupted program has no mechanism by which it could detect that. Every async thing you have written bottoms out here."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "What is the IDT and how is it used?", "The Interrupt Descriptor Table: 256 entries on x86, each holding a handler address, with the <code>idtr</code> register pointing at it. The processor uses the interrupt number as an array index. It is a table lookup, nothing more."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "Name the three kinds of entry into the kernel that share the IDT.", "<strong>Hardware interrupts</strong> (a device raised a line — asynchronous), <strong>exceptions</strong> (the current instruction could not complete: divide by zero, invalid opcode, page fault — synchronous), and <strong>software interrupts</strong> (a deliberate trap, i.e. a system call). Every transition into the kernel is one of these three."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "What is an IRQ and why were conflicts a thing in the 1990s?", "An interrupt request line number, from the Intel 8259 PIC's eight inputs (fifteen when chained). There were only fifteen, so devices had to share or conflict. Modern machines use APIC and PCIe MSI-X, where the signal is a write to a special memory address, allowing dozens of vectors per device."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "Why must interrupts be maskable, and what is a critical section?", "Because an interrupt can arrive while a data structure is midway through a multi-step update, and a handler that walks it sees an inconsistent state. <code>cli</code>/<code>sti</code> bracket the region — a critical section. The same problem reappears as every race condition and every database isolation level."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "What are top halves and bottom halves, and where do you see the cost?", "The top half is the actual handler: acknowledge the device, note there is work, return. The bottom half — softirqs, tasklets, work queues — does the real processing later with interrupts enabled. You see it as the <code>si</code> column in <code>top</code>; high <code>si</code> on one core is unbalanced interrupt handling."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "What is receive livelock, and what fixes it?", "At over a million packets per second the machine does nothing but enter and leave interrupt handlers. The fix inverts the node: the driver disables interrupts and polls, because at high rates polling always finds work. Linux's mechanism is NAPI, switching modes automatically."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "State the general rule about interrupts versus polling.", "Interrupts win when events are rare; polling wins when events are dense. The same trade decides WebSocket push versus HTTP polling, database triggers versus batch scans, and filesystem watching versus periodic scanning."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "What is DMA and what does it imply about a network interrupt?", "Direct Memory Access: the device writes to main memory itself while the processor does something else. So an interrupt from a network card means <em>the packet is already in memory</em>, not that one is available for fetching. It is also why zero-copy techniques target the remaining software copies."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "Trace a packet from the wire to a JavaScript callback.", "NIC reassembles the frame → DMA into a ring buffer → MSI-X interrupt → driver top half schedules a NET_RX softirq → softirq walks Ethernet/IP/TCP and appends to a socket buffer → the socket's wait-queue callback adds its descriptor to an epoll ready list → your process's <code>epoll_wait</code> returns → libuv invokes the C callback → <code>read()</code> → HTTP parser → V8 → your handler."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "Your callback ran. Why did it run?", "Because a wire went high. The event loop is not the origin of asynchrony in your system; it is software that organises asynchrony the hardware creates."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "What is interrupt coalescing and when does it show up in your measurements?", "Network cards deliberately delay interrupts by tens of microseconds to batch packets (<code>ethtool -c</code>). It contributes to the gap when a client-observed latency exceeds server-side timing plus network transit — together with Nagle's algorithm and delayed ACK, whose interaction produces the classic 40 ms stall that <code>setNoDelay(true)</code> addresses."),
("node-2", "Node 2 · The interrupt", "How is a webhook endpoint that queues a job and returns 202 the same pattern as a network driver?", "It is a top half: get out of the handler fast because the caller times out, and do the real work where you are allowed to take your time. Same pressure, same design, different scale."),
# ---------- Node 3 ----------
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "Why can protection not be implemented in software?", "Whatever check you write runs as instructions the offending program could jump past, overwrite, or never call. A guard the guarded party can remove is not a guard. Enforcement must be in hardware, below the level any instruction can reach."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "What is the mode bit?", "One bit inside the processor — not in memory — holding whether execution is in kernel mode (ring 0) or user mode (ring 3). Certain instructions are declared privileged and raise an exception if decoded in user mode."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "Name five privileged instructions on x86-64 and say what each protects.", "<code>cli</code>/<code>sti</code> (interrupts, so a program cannot freeze the machine), <code>lidt</code> (the interrupt table, so it cannot redirect handlers), <code>mov cr3</code> (the page table root, so it cannot remap memory), <code>in</code>/<code>out</code> (hardware ports, so it cannot drive the disk), <code>hlt</code> (stopping the processor)."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "Why does the mode bit alone not give isolation?", "An ordinary <code>mov</code> is not privileged, so a user program could still write over kernel memory. Hardware must also check <em>which addresses</em> a program may touch, on every access, from tables only the kernel can modify — first base-and-limit registers, later page tables."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "What is a segmentation fault, physically?", "A memory access the hardware refused, raising an exception, whose kernel handler delivers <code>SIGSEGV</code>, whose default action terminates the process. The word segmentation is a fossil from an older memory scheme; the mechanism today is paging."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "State the correct model of what the kernel is.", "<strong>Not a process — a mode the processor is in.</strong> Your process executes kernel code, on its own time slice, with the privilege bit raised, and returns. There is no separate kernel process being scheduled."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "Given that model, what do <code>us</code> and <code>sy</code> in <code>top</code> mean?", "<code>us</code> is time your process spent executing with the mode bit at user; <code>sy</code> is time <em>your process</em> spent executing kernel code on its own behalf. Both are charged to it — which is why <code>process.cpuUsage()</code> returns user and system separately."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "Why does a kernel bug panic the machine while a user-space bug kills one process?", "Because the failing code <em>is</em> the system; there is no process to terminate. That asymmetry is the entire argument for microkernels."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "What was Multics and why does its failure matter?", "A 1965 MIT/Bell Labs/GE project for a computing utility shared by mutually untrusting users, with eight hardware-checked protection rings. It was enormous and late; Bell Labs withdrew in 1969, and Thompson and Ritchie then wrote Unix in reaction. Every system you deploy to descends from that withdrawal."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "x86 has four rings. Why does everyone use two?", "The paging hardware only distinguishes supervisor from user anyway, and portable kernels cannot rely on an x86-only feature. Rings 1 and 2 sat unused for thirty years until early paravirtualised Xen; hardware virtualisation later added an effective ring −1 for hypervisors."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "Why is there no JavaScript that opens a file?", "Opening a file requires talking to a storage controller, which requires privileged instructions, which user-mode code cannot execute. Every capability your runtime appears to have is a request across this boundary, and the complete list of what it can ask for is the syscall table."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "Why can <code>process.on('uncaughtException')</code> not save you from a crash in a native addon?", "A bad pointer raises a hardware exception, delivered as <code>SIGSEGV</code>, and the process dies below the level at which V8 has any say. There is no JavaScript stack to unwind because the failure was not in JavaScript."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "Why does Chrome run one process per site, and what did it cost?", "Because Spectre showed that software separation inside one process (V8 isolates and contexts) is no defence against a hardware side channel. Site Isolation, fully shipped in Chrome 67 (May 2018), moves the boundary to the hardware one, at roughly 10–13% more memory. It is also why <code>SharedArrayBuffer</code> requires COOP and COEP headers."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "How is WebAssembly's security model a re-derivation of this node?", "A Wasm module has one linear memory, every access bounds-checked, no way to form a pointer outside it, and no instruction that reaches the host — only imported functions the host chose to provide, which is a syscall table built in software. WASI makes the resemblance total."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "What does <code>--privileged</code> actually grant?", "It stops dropping the capabilities that let a process reconfigure the kernel: loading modules, raw device access, changing interrupt configuration. It is close to giving the container the host. The right fix for a container needing one ability is a single <code>--cap-add</code>."),
("node-3", "Node 3 · Privilege", "eBPF runs user-supplied code in kernel mode. How is that reconciled with this node's rule?", "With a <strong>verifier</strong>: the kernel statically proves the bytecode terminates, that every memory access is in bounds, and that it calls only permitted helpers, before JIT-compiling it. A proof obligation substituting for a hardware boundary."),
# ---------- Node 4 ----------
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "What is the asymmetry that makes the syscall design work?", "The user program controls <em>when</em> the crossing happens; it does not control <em>where</em> it lands. One instruction switches the mode bit and jumps to one address the kernel registered in advance."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "Give the Linux x86-64 syscall ABI.", "Number in <code>rax</code>; arguments in <code>rdi</code>, <code>rsi</code>, <code>rdx</code>, <code>r10</code>, <code>r8</code>, <code>r9</code>; the <code>syscall</code> instruction; result in <code>rax</code>, where a value between −1 and −4095 is a negated errno."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "Write <code>console.log(\"hi\")</code> as machine-level operations.", "<code>mov rax,1</code> (write); <code>mov rdi,1</code> (stdout); <code>mov rsi,message</code>; <code>mov rdx,2</code>; <code>syscall</code>. Every layer above — V8, Node bindings, libuv, glibc — exists to arrange those five lines."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "Why must the kernel switch to a separate stack on entry?", "Trusting the user's stack pointer would let a program point it at kernel memory. Each process has a small kernel stack (typically 16 KB) used only while executing kernel code on its behalf."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "Why does the kernel never dereference a user pointer directly?", "A program can pass any address, including a kernel address, so a direct dereference would let any process read kernel memory via <code>read()</code>. All user memory goes through <code>copy_from_user</code>/<code>copy_to_user</code>, which validate ranges and handle faults — the most security-critical code in the kernel."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "What does a syscall cost, and what made it worse?", "Roughly 50–100 ns. KPTI, the Meltdown mitigation from January 2018, unmaps kernel memory from the user page table so every crossing also reloads the page table root and disturbs address translation — five to thirty percent on syscall-heavy workloads."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "What is Linux's stable ABI, and how does Windows differ?", "On Linux the <strong>syscall interface</strong> is permanent — Torvalds's rule that userspace is never broken. On Windows syscall numbers change between builds and the stable contract is one level up in <code>ntdll.dll</code>/<code>kernel32.dll</code>. This is why a static Go binary works in a <code>FROM scratch</code> container."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "Distinguish a library function from a system call, with an example of each and one that is both.", "<code>strlen</code> runs entirely in user mode. <code>write</code> must enter the kernel. <code>printf</code> is a library function that formats into a user buffer and calls <code>write</code> when the buffer fills or a newline appears."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "Why do logs sometimes stop just before a crash?", "Buffering. Output to a pipe or file is fully buffered (typically 4 KB) rather than line-buffered as to a terminal, so the last chunk is lost when the process dies abruptly. Every language has a version of this."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "State the principle that explains buffered I/O, epoll, sendfile, writev and io_uring at once.", "A system call costs far more than an instruction, so software batches work in user space to make fewer of them."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "Map five Node APIs to the syscalls they make.", "<code>fs.readFile</code> → <code>openat</code>/<code>read</code>/<code>close</code> on a threadpool thread; <code>net</code>/<code>http</code> → <code>socket</code>/<code>bind</code>/<code>listen</code>/<code>accept4</code>/<code>read</code>/<code>write</code> driven by epoll; <code>setTimeout</code> → no syscall, just the timeout argument to <code>epoll_wait</code>; <code>spawn</code> → <code>clone</code>/<code>execve</code>/<code>pipe2</code>/<code>waitpid</code>; <code>crypto.pbkdf2</code> → no syscall at all, pure computation on the threadpool."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "Why is <code>process.stdout</code> synchronous to a terminal and asynchronous to a pipe?", "A terminal or file will not block for long, so Node does the <code>write</code> inline. A pipe can block indefinitely on a slow reader, so it is made non-blocking and buffered. Consequences: heavy logging in a terminal costs a syscall per call, and output can be lost on an abrupt exit under Docker where stdout is a pipe."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "What is seccomp and how does Docker use it?", "A filter — a small kernel-checked program — inspecting each syscall's number and arguments to allow, deny or kill. Docker's default profile blocks about 44 of ~350 calls including <code>mount</code> and module loading, which is why some tooling gets an unexplained <code>EPERM</code>. Kubernetes exposes it as <code>seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault</code>."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "How does gVisor work, and why is it slower?", "It implements the Linux syscall interface in a user-mode process (Sentry) and services a container's calls there, so an escape must break Sentry first. It works because this interface is the <em>complete</em> boundary. It is slower because each syscall becomes several real crossings."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "Give a concrete case where syscall count is the performance problem.", "<code>fs.appendFileSync</code> per log line makes <code>openat</code>, <code>write</code> and <code>close</code> each time — three crossings per line. Holding the descriptor and batching makes one per batch. At scale this is the difference between logging costing one percent of your loop and twenty."),
("node-4", "Node 4 · The system call", "What does <code>strace -c -f</code> tell you that a profiler will not?", "How many of each system call the process made and how much time went into each. An idle Node server is one blocked <code>epoll_wait</code>; a loaded one is <code>epoll_wait</code>, a burst of <code>read</code>/<code>write</code>/<code>futex</code>, and back. It is the runtime made visible."),
]
